<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis IX of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1214">1214</a>–<a href="/years/1270">1270</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Saint Louis · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">Capetian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis IX of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, king from 1226 to 1270, was revered as a model Christian ruler and canonised in 1297. He led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seventh-crusade">Seventh Crusade</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where he was captured, then spent years refortifying crusader towns such as Caesarea. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1259">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1259 he settled the dispute with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and he died besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighth-crusade">Eighth Crusade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france.jpg" alt="Louis IX of France" width="320" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saintlouis_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The intervention of the French crown proved decisive: Louis VIII’s crusade of 1226 broke resistance, and by the Treaty of Paris of 1229 Raymond VII kept a reduced county for life, while his only daughter Joan was married to Alphonse of Poitiers, brother of <b>Louis IX</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Paris of 1259 Henry III of England gave up his claims to Normandy, Anjou and Poitou and did homage to <b>Louis IX</b> for a duchy reduced to Gascony, or Guyenne, with promises of lands in the Limousin, Périgord, Quercy and Agenais.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…III commanded the French contingent of the Third Crusade after Philip II returned home in 1191 and died at Acre in 1192, Odo III fought for Philip II at the Battle of Bouvines in 1214, and Hugh IV joined the crusade of <b>Louis IX</b> to Egypt in 1248–1250.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty’s greatest test came in 1270, when <b>Louis IX of France</b> landed at Carthage on what became the Eighth Crusade; disease ravaged the camp, Louis died near Tunis in August, and Charles of Anjou negotiated a treaty in which al-Mustansir paid an indemnity and renewed tribute to…</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles of Anjou, brother of <b>Louis IX of France</b>, acquired the County of Provence by marrying its heiress Beatrice in 1246, bringing it into the orbit of the French royal family and later of the Angevin kings of Naples.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Louis IX</b> the king's court produced the Parlement of Paris, the supreme court of appeal, and a Chamber of Accounts followed in the early fourteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
